use std
into locks.amni

Imports: math, gfx, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.shapes, gfx.hair and gfx.hairdo. Namespace locks. Card hair: every guide of a groom becomes a flat textured lock that follows a smooth curve through the guide, lies flat on the head and splits into ragged ends, plus an optional inner layer of wider, shorter cards. The head is the one `hairdo` measured: `hairdo::ctr` (float3, its center) and `hairdo::keep(p float3, gap f32) float3` (pushes a point out of the head to at least `gap` above it) and `hairdo::radius(d float3) f32` (the head's radius along a unit direction from `hairdo::ctr`) already exist; never define them. Read groom bytes only with `hair::u32_at(data, at) int` and `hair::f32_at(data, at) f32`, which already exist (never write byte readers here). Groom bytes ("HMG" format): np as a u32 at byte 8, ns as a u32 at byte 12 (read little-endian), np points of 48 bytes from byte 16 (floats: position x, y, z at 0, 4, 8; the side vector, whose length is the half width, at 16, 20, 24), then ns segments of 16 bytes from 16 plus np times 48, each starting with the i32 point index of its first point (the segment joins that point and the next).

A struct `Spec` with fields `sub` (int, curve samples per guide segment, default 3), `width` (real, card width scale, 1.0), `split` (real, share of the length where ends split, 0.7), `forks` (int, ends per lock, 3), `spread` (real, how far the ends fan apart, 2.0), `ragged` (real, how much end lengths vary, 0.2), `flat` (bool, lie flat on the head, true), `inner` (bool, add the inner layer, true), `gap` (real, meters kept above the head, 0.0015), `verts` (int, the vertex budget, 110000). `spec() Spec` returns the defaults.

Module vars, the result of `build`: `P`, `N` and `T` (f32, three per vertex: position, normal, tangent along the hair from root to tip), `UV` (f32, two per vertex), `I` (u32, three per triangle); per row (every row is two vertices, left then right): `row_v` (int, its first vertex), `row_lock` (int, its guide), `row_fork` (int: minus 1 for the lock's trunk, else the fork number), `row_layer` (int: 0 outer, 1 inner), `row_guide` (int: the groom point index when the row sits exactly on a guide point, else minus 1). Also `split_row` (int per lock: the row index in the row arrays of the trunk's split row, minus 1 when the lock does not split) and `fork_row` (int, `forks` per lock: the row index of each fork's first row).

`hash01(a int, b int) real` gives a repeatable pseudo-random number from 0 to 1 below: h is a times 73856093 bitwise xor b times 19349663, then h xor (h shifted right by 13), times 1274126177, xor (h shifted right by 16), keeping the low 31 bits; the result is that over 2147483648.

`smooth(a real, b real, x real) real` is the smoothstep of x from a to b (t clamped 0 to 1, then t times t times (3 minus 2 t)).

`profile(t real) real` is the width along a lock: (0.65 plus 0.35 times `smooth(0, 0.2, t)`) times (1 minus 0.55 times `smooth(0.55, 1, t)`).

`emit(p float3, side float3, hw real, nrm float3, tg float3, u0 real, u1 real, v real, s Spec, lock int, fork int, layer int, guide int)` adds one row: the left vertex at p minus side times hw and the right at p plus side times hw, each passed through `hairdo::keep` with a gap (as f32): for layer 0 the smaller of `s.gap` and the larger of 0 and the center's clearance (its distance from `hairdo::ctr` minus `hairdo::radius` of the unit direction from `hairdo::ctr` to it), so a card is never pushed further from the head than its own curve; for layer 1 `s.gap`; both get normal nrm, tangent tg and v; u is u0 on the left and u1 on the right. Push the row's bookkeeping (`row_v` is the vertex count before adding) and, when this row is not the first row of its strip (the previous row has the same lock, fork and layer and is the row just before it), two triangles from the previous row's left a, right b and this row's left c, right d: (a, c, b) and (b, c, d).

`build(data, s Spec) int` (data a read-only byte slice) clears every result array and returns 0 when data is shorter than 16 bytes, then collects the guides: walk the segments in order; a guide starts at a segment whose first point is not the previous segment's first point plus 1, and its points are that first point and the next point of each of its consecutive segments. Budget: with n guides and k the first guide's point count, R = (k minus 1) times `s.sub` plus 1, rs as below, the rows per lock are rs plus 1, plus `s.forks` times (1 plus the ceiling of (R minus 1 minus rs) over 2) when `s.forks` is above 1, plus 2 plus the integer part of the rounded value of 0.85 times (R minus 1) over 2 when `s.inner`; kept = the smaller of n and the integer part of `s.verts` over (2 times the rows per lock), at least 1; scale = the square root of n over kept. Guide i is used when kept equals n or `hash01(i, 77)` is below kept over n. For each used guide, as `lock` counting from 0, with its k points c (positions) and half widths w (the side vector's length times `s.width` times scale), when k is at least 2:
- Curve: rows R = (k minus 1) times `s.sub` plus 1. Row r with j = r over `s.sub` (integer) and f = (r mod `s.sub`) over `s.sub`; for the last row j is k minus 2 and f is 1. Its center is the uniform Catmull-Rom point between c[j] and c[j+1] at f, using the point before (c[j-1], or 2 c[j] minus c[j+1] at the start) and after (c[j+2], or 2 c[j+1] minus c[j] at the end); its half width interpolates w[j] to w[j+1] linearly at f, times `profile(r over (R minus 1))`; it sits on guide point (the groom index of c[j], or of c[j+1] when f is 1) when f is 0 or r is the last row, else minus 1.
- Frame per row: tangent the unit vector from the previous row's center to the next one's (the row itself at the ends); out the unit vector from `hairdo::ctr` to the center. Side: when `s.flat`, the unit cross product of tangent and out, else of tangent and (1, 0, 0); when that cross product's length is below 1e-6 use the cross product of tangent and (0, 0, 1). Card normal: the unit cross product of side and tangent; when `s.flat` and its dot product with out is negative, negate both side and normal. The vertex normal is the unit vector of the card normal plus out times 0.5 (the card normal alone when not `s.flat`).
- Split row rs: when `s.forks` is above 1, rs is the rounded value of `s.split` times (R minus 1) clamped to 1 to R minus 2; else rs is R minus 1 (no split).
- Trunk: `emit` rows 0 to rs (inclusive) with u 0 to 1, v r over (R minus 1), fork minus 1, layer 0, its guide point. Store the trunk's split row (its index in the row arrays) in `split_row`, or minus 1 when `s.forks` is 1 or less.
- Forks (when `s.forks` is above 1), F = `s.forks`, M = the ceiling of (R minus 1 minus rs) over 2 (forks use half as many rows), t0 = rs over (R minus 1): for each fork f from 0, its end parameter te is the larger of t0 plus 0.05 times (1 minus t0) and 1 minus `s.ragged` times `hash01(lock, f)`; c_f = minus 1 plus (2 f plus 1) over F. Record the fork's first row index in `fork_row`. For m from 0 to M: x = m over M; the parameter t = t0 plus (te minus t0) times x; look up the trunk curve at t by linear interpolation between rows (row position t times (R minus 1)) of center, half width, side, card normal, vertex normal and tangent (directions scaled back to length one); g = 1 plus `s.spread` times `smooth(0, 1, x)`; the fork center is the center plus side times half width times c_f times g; its half width is the half width over F times (1 plus 0.15 times `smooth(0, 1, x)`) times (1 minus 0.7 times `smooth(0.75, 1, x)`); `emit` it with u from f over F to (f plus 1) over F, v t, fork f, layer 0, guide minus 1. At m 0 these equal the trunk's split row exactly.
- Inner layer when `s.inner`, with re the rounded value of 0.85 times (R minus 1): first a root tuck row at row 0's center minus row 0's tangent times 3 times row 0's half width, then the rows r = 0, 2, 4 and so on up to re, and row re itself when re is odd; each row (the tuck uses row 0's frame) has its center moved by minus the card normal times 0.003, half width times 1.3, u 0 to 1, v r over (R minus 1) (0 for the tuck), fork minus 1, layer 1, guide minus 1.
Return the lock count.

`strands(size int, seed int) int` makes the strand texture and returns its index from `gltf::add_texture_rgba(px, size, size, 10497)`: px is size times size RGBA bytes, row y at v = (y plus 0.5) over size (row 0 the root), starting with red, green and blue 224 and alpha 0. Draw the rounded value of 0.75 times size strands; strand j has x center `hash01(seed, 4 j)` times size, width 0.6 plus `hash01(seed, 4 j plus 1)` (pixels), length 0.8 plus 0.2 times `hash01(seed, 4 j plus 2)` (as a share of the height; when `hash01(seed, 4 j plus 3)` is below 0.15 it is 0.55 plus 0.25 times that hash over 0.15 instead), brightness 0.75 plus 0.25 times `hash01(seed, 4 j plus 3)`. For each row whose v is below the strand's length and each column within its width plus 1 of the center (wrapping around the edges): coverage is the clamp to 0 to 1 of (width over 2 plus 0.5 minus the distance between the column's center x plus 0.5 and the strand center, measured the short way around), times 1 minus `smooth(0.85 times length, length, v)`; when the coverage is above that pixel's current alpha over 255, set its alpha to coverage times 255 and its red, green and blue to 255 times brightness times (0.82 plus 0.18 times `smooth(0, 0.3, v)`). Finally scale every pixel's alpha by `smooth(0, 0.08, u)` times `smooth(0, 0.08, 1 minus u)` with u = (column plus 0.5) over size. Values rounded and clamped to 0 to 255.

`material(r f32, g f32, b f32, tex int) gltf::Material` is `shapes::material` of (r, g, b), metallic 0, roughness 0.45, with `base_tex` a `gltf::TexRef` of tex and texture coordinate set 0, `alpha_mode` `gltf::ALPHA_MASK`, `alpha_cutoff` 0.35, `double_sided` true, `shading` 7, `shade_a` (0.4, 0.08, 0.25, 0.3) and `shade_c` (-0.08, 0.12, 120, 30).

`scalp(data, P, r real)` (data a read-only byte slice, the groom; P a read-only f32 slice, three per point) clears the module array `scalp_w` (f32) and pushes one weight per point of P. The roots: np is the u32 at byte 8 of data and ns the u32 at byte 12 (read with `hair::u32_at`); segment k's first point index is the u32 at byte 16 plus 48 times np plus 16 times k, and that point's position the three f32 at byte 16 plus 48 times the index (`hair::f32_at`). With no roots (or data shorter than 16 bytes) every weight is 0. Bin the roots in a uniform grid over their bounding box grown by r on every side, with cell size c the larger of r and the box's largest side over 64 (so at most 66 cells a side): a count per cell, a running start per cell and the root numbers sorted by cell. For each point, look at the cells within the ceiling of r over c of the point's cell (clamped to the grid; points outside the grown box get 0), find the smallest distance d to a root, and push 1 minus smoothstep(r over 2, r, d) (1 when d is at most r over 2, 0 when at least r).

`mesh(m gltf::Material) int` adds the result as a scene asset: remember the vertex count of `gltf::verts` (its length over `gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`) as v0 and `gltf::indices` length as i0; `shapes::vertex(position, normal, u, v, tangent with w 1)` for every vertex (all as float3 and f32) while tracking the box, then set that vertex's color red, green and blue (offsets `gltf::AT_COL`, plus 1 and plus 2, in `gltf::verts`) to k, where k is 1 plus `jitter` times (2 times `hash01(lock, 31)` minus 1), with lock the `row_lock` of the vertex's row (vertex index over 2 is its row, since every row is two vertices) and `jitter` a module var (real, 0.18); push every index of `I` unchanged to `gltf::indices` (indices are local to the mesh, counting from its first vertex); return `shapes::finish(v0, i0, lo, hi, m)`.
